var definitions = ['<h2>Pablo y Carmen son amigos.</h2><div><strong>Pablo</strong> This is a proper noun, a male given name in Spanish.</div><div><strong>y</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish, equivalent to "and" in English, used to connect words or phrases that appear in a list or series.</div><div><strong>Carmen</strong> This is also a proper noun, a female given name in Spanish.</div><div><strong>son</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "ser" in Spanish, which means "to be". It indicates a state of being, in this case "are", and denotes a relationship or a characteristic of the subjects (Pablo and Carmen).</div><div><strong>amigos</strong> This is a noun in Spanish meaning "friends". It is a masculine plural noun that matches in number and gender with the subjects, Pablo and Carmen. If referring to a group of female friends, it would be "amigas".</div>','<h2>Es accesible y de buena calidad.</h2><div><strong>Es</strong> Este es el verbo ser en español en la tercera persona del singular del presente de indicativo. Se usa para describir una característica o estado permanente del sujeto.</div><div><strong>accesible</strong> Este es un adjetivo en español que describe algo a lo cual se puede acceder fácilmente o que está al alcance de las personas. Aquí, se refiere a la disponibilidad o facilidad de uso del sujeto no mencionado.</div><div><strong>y</strong> Esta es la conjunción copulativa en español que significa "and" en inglés. Se utiliza para unir dos ideas relacionadas.</div><div><strong>de</strong> Esta es una preposición en español que puede indicar posesión o formar parte de una expresión que describe características.</div><div><strong>buena</strong> Este es un adjetivo calificativo en español que significa "good" en inglés. Se utiliza para describir la calidad de algo y está en forma femenina para concordar con el sustantivo "calidad".</div><div><strong>calidad</strong> Este es un sustantivo femenino en español que se refiere al nivel de excelencia de algo. Aquí, significa que el sujeto al que se refiere cumple con estándares positivos o deseables.</div>','<h2>Hay salsa de tomate y mostaza en su perro caliente.</h2><div><strong>Hay</strong> This word comes from the verb "haber" and is equivalent to "there is" or "there are" in English. It’s used to indicate existence or presence.</div><div><strong>salsa</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "sauce". It could refer to any type of sauce but is often used in a culinary context.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish which is translated to "of" or "from" in English. In this sentence, it’s used to connect the noun "salsa" with what type of sauce it is.</div><div><strong>tomate</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "tomato". Combined with "de", it specifies the kind of sauce mentioned.</div><div><strong>y</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish that means "and". It’s used to link words and phrases together.</div><div><strong>mostaza</strong> This noun in Spanish refers to "mustard". It’s mentioned as another type of sauce in the list.</div><div><strong>en</strong> This preposition in Spanish means "in" or "on". It indicates the location or placement of something.</div><div><strong>su</strong> This possessive adjective means "your" in English. It’s used to indicate ownership or association to the subject you are speaking to.</div><div><strong>perro caliente</strong> This is a Spanish term for "hot dog", which is a common food item consisting of a sausage in a bun. It’s the noun phrase to which the sauces are being applied.</div>','<h2>En la cesta hay manzanas y naranjas.</h2><div><strong>En</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that translates to "in" in English. It indicates the location or position of something.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the singular, feminine form of the definite article in Spanish, meaning "the". It agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies.</div><div><strong>cesta</strong> This is a feminine noun in Spanish that means "basket". Here, it is where the fruits are located.</div><div><strong>hay</strong> This comes from the verb "haber" in Spanish. The third-person singular present form is used to express the existence of something, similar to "there is" or "there are" in English.</div><div><strong>manzanas</strong> This is a plural noun in Spanish that translates to "apples" in English. It follows "cesta" as one of the items contained within it.</div><div><strong>y</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish that means "and". It is used to connect words or phrases that are to be taken jointly.</div><div><strong>naranjas</strong> This is another plural noun in Spanish meaning "oranges". It follows the conjunction "y", being the second item listed in the basket.</div>','<h2>Tengo un gato y un perro.</h2><div><strong>Tengo</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "tener", which means "to have" in English. It indicates possession or ownership by the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the Spanish indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>gato</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "cat". It is masculine and singular to match the article "un".</div><div><strong>y</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It is a conjunction used to connect words or phrases of the same part of speech.</div><div><strong>un</strong> (repeated) Again, this is the Spanish indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English, used before the second noun in the series.</div><div><strong>perro</strong> This is another Spanish noun meaning "dog". Similarly to "gato", it is masculine and singular, matching the preceding article "un".</div>','<h2>¿Quieres ir al cine o al restaurante?</h2><div><strong>¿Quieres</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "querer", which means "to want". It is used here to ask "Do you want".</div><div><strong>ir</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "ir", which means "to go" in English. It is used in conjunction with "quieres" to form the phrase "Do you want to go".</div><div><strong>al</strong> This is a contraction of the preposition "a" (to) and the definite article "el" (the), which together mean "to the" when referring to a masculine singular noun in Spanish. </div><div><strong>cine</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "cinema" or "movie theater". It is a masculine singular noun, which is why "al" is used before it. </div><div><strong>o</strong> This is the Spanish word for "or". It is a conjunction used to offer alternatives or choices.</div><div><strong>al restaurante?</strong> This phrase combines "al" (to the) again with "restaurante", which is a masculine singular noun meaning "restaurant". The question mark at the end denotes that the sentence is a question, asking if the person would like to go to either the cinema or the restaurant.</div>','<h2>¿Qué comerás, la manzana o el pastel?</h2><div><strong>¿Qué</strong> This is a Spanish interrogative pronoun meaning "what". It is used to ask for specific information and here it introduces a question about a choice of food.</div><div><strong>comerás</strong> This is the second-person singular future tense form of the verb "comer", which means "to eat". It indicates an action that the subject will perform in the future.</div><div><strong>la manzana</strong> This phrase is composed of the definite article "la" (the) and the noun "manzana" (apple). Together, they refer to "the apple", which is one of the food options being presented.</div><div><strong>o</strong> This is a Spanish conjunction meaning "or". It is used to present alternative options or choices.</div><div><strong>el pastel</strong> This phrase combines the definite article "el" (the) with the noun "pastel" (cake). Together, they signify "the cake", which is the second option available in this context.</div>','<h2>¿Quieres el reloj negro o el blanco?</h2><div><strong>¿Quieres</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "querer", which means "to want". Here, it forms a question implying "Do you want".</div><div><strong>el</strong> This is the definite article in Spanish for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>reloj</strong> This is a Spanish noun that means "watch" or "clock". It is masculine singular and is the object of the verb "quieres".</div><div><strong>negro</strong> This is a Spanish adjective meaning "black". It is in masculine singular form to agree with "reloj".</div><div><strong>o</strong> This word is the Spanish conjunction for "or", used to offer a choice between two options.</div><div><strong>el</strong> Again, this is the definite article in Spanish for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It’s repeated here to clarify the second option.</div><div><strong>blanco</strong> This is a Spanish adjective meaning "white". It is in masculine singular form to agree with "reloj" and it represents the second choice of color for the watch.</div><div><strong>?</strong> This punctuation denotes that the sentence is a question in Spanish, just as in English.</div>','<h2>¿Cuál prefiere? ¿El gris o el blanco?</h2><div><strong>¿Cuál</strong> This is an interrogative pronoun in Spanish, meaning "which" or "what". It is used here to ask for a preference between options.</div><div><strong>prefiere?</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "preferir", which means "to prefer". It is conjugated to match an implied formal "you" (usted) as the subject in the question.</div><div><strong>¿El gris</strong> This is a noun phrase that means "the gray one" in English. "El" is the masculine singular definite article "the", and "gris" is the color "gray" used here as a noun.</div><div><strong>o</strong> This is the Spanish word for "or". It is used to offer a choice between two options.</div><div><strong>el blanco?</strong> This is a noun phrase that means "the white one" in English. "El" is the masculine singular definite article "the", and "blanco" is the color "white" used here as a noun.</div>','<h2>Él también tiene mucho talento.</h2><div><strong>Él</strong> This is the Spanish third-person singular pronoun that means "he". It is used to refer to a male subject in the sentence.</div><div><strong>también</strong> This is a Spanish adverb meaning "also" or "too". It indicates that the subject possesses a quality that has already been attributed to someone else.</div><div><strong>tiene</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "tener", which means "to have". In this context, it indicates possession or presence of a characteristic.</div><div><strong>mucho</strong> This is a Spanish adjective that translates to "much" or "a lot of". It amplifies the degree of the noun it modifies, in this case, "talento".</div><div><strong>talento</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "talent". It refers to someone’s natural aptitude or skill.</div>','<h2>Ella también corre a menudo en el parque.</h2><div><strong>Ella</strong> This is a Spanish pronoun for "she". It refers to a female subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>también</strong> This is a Spanish adverb meaning "also" or "too". It indicates that the subject shares an attribute or action with someone or something else mentioned within the context.</div><div><strong>corre</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "correr", which means "to run". It indicates the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>a menudo</strong> This Spanish phrase translates to "often" in English. It indicates the frequency at which the action is performed.</div><div><strong>en</strong> This is a Spanish preposition meaning "in" or "on". It indicates the location where the action is taking place.</div><div><strong>el parque</strong> This phrase means "the park" in English. It’s a combination of the definite article "el" (the) and the noun "parque" (park), specifying the place where she runs.</div>','<h2>¡Yo también te quiero!</h2><div><strong>¡Yo</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, emphasizing the action the speaker is taking or feeling.</div><div><strong>también</strong> This is a Spanish adverb meaning "also" or "too". It is used to agree with or reciprocate a sentiment or action.</div><div><strong>te</strong> This is the second-person singular object pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "you" in English. It indicates that the action of the verb is directed towards the person being spoken to.</div><div><strong>quiero</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "querer", which means "to love" or "to want". In this context, it means "I love" as an expression of affection towards the second person.</div>','<h2>Ella come frutas también.</h2><div><strong>Ella</strong> This is the third-person singular feminine pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>come</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "comer", which means "to eat". It indicates the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>frutas</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "fruits". It’s the direct object of the sentence, showing what the subject eats.</div><div><strong>también</strong> This is a Spanish adverb meaning "also" or "too". It is used to add information to the sentence, indicating that the action of eating fruits is in addition to other actions or habits previously mentioned or understood.</div>'];var defBonus = ['<h2>¿Y adónde va?</h2><div><strong>¿Y</strong> This is the Spanish word for "and". It is often used at the beginning of a question to continue or follow up on a previous statement or question.</div><div><strong>adónde</strong> This is a contraction of the preposition "a" (to) and the interrogative adverb "dónde" (where), forming the interrogative adverb "adónde", which means "to where". This is used to ask about the destination of someone or something.</div><div><strong>va?</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "ir", which means "to go". Here it is used to ask where the person is going.</div>','<h2>¿Y qué edad tienen?</h2><div><strong>¿Y</strong> This is the Spanish word for "and". It is used to connect words or phrases, similar to the English word "and". In questions, it can also serve to continue a line of questioning or add a related question.</div><div><strong>qué</strong> This word translates to "what" in English. It is commonly used to ask for information specifying something, and it’s used here to inquire about an age.</div><div><strong>edad</strong> This is the Spanish noun for "age". It refers to the duration of existence from birth of a person or object.</div><div><strong>tienen</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "tener", which means "to have" in English. In questions, it is used here to ask about the possession of a certain age (how old they are).</div>','<h2>¿Y todavía tiene gasolina?</h2><div><strong>¿</strong> This symbol is the Spanish opening question mark. It is used at the beginning of a question.</div><div><strong>Y</strong> This is the Spanish word for "and". It is used as a conjunction to connect words, phrases, or clauses.</div><div><strong>todavía</strong> This Spanish adverb means "still" or "yet". It indicates that an action or a state continues up to a certain time.</div><div><strong>tiene</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "tener", which means "to have" in English. Here it is used to refer to an unspecified subject, and it could also refer to the formal second person (usted).</div><div><strong>gasolina</strong> This is the Spanish noun for "gasoline" or "petrol". It’s what fuels most internal combustion engines.</div><div><strong>?</strong> This symbol is the Spanish closing question mark. It is used at the end of a question to indicate that it is a question.</div>','<h2>Solamente para mi mujer y para mí.</h2><div><strong>Solamente</strong> This is a Spanish adverb meaning "only" or "just". It is used to restrict the context or scope of the statement to what’s specified after it.</div><div><strong>para</strong> This is a Spanish preposition that can be translated as "for". It is used twice in this sentence to indicate the intended recipient or beneficiary of an action or object.</div><div><strong>mi mujer</strong> This phrase consists of a possessive adjective <em>mi</em> meaning "my", and a noun <em>mujer</em> meaning "wife" or "woman". Here, it’s specifying the speaker’s wife as one of the intended recipients.</div><div><strong>y</strong> This is the Spanish conjunction for "and". It’s used here to connect the two recipients, the speaker’s wife and the speaker themself.</div><div><strong>para mí</strong> This phrase repeats the preposition <em>para</em>, followed by the first-person singular pronoun <em>mí</em> meaning "me" or "myself". This denotes the speaker as the other intended recipient.</div>','<h2>¿Y cuándo desea regresar?</h2><div><strong>¿</strong> This symbol is the inverted question mark used to begin a question in Spanish. It indicates that what follows is a question.</div><div><strong>Y</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It is a conjunction used to connect phrases or clauses.</div><div><strong>cuándo</strong> This is an interrogative pronoun in Spanish meaning "when". It is used to ask about a point in time.</div><div><strong>desea</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "desear", which means "to wish" or "to want". In this context, it can be understood as "do you wish" or "do you want", because it’s part of a question directed at the second-person polite "you" (usted).</div><div><strong>regresar</strong> This is an infinitive verb in Spanish that translates to "return" or "come back" in English. It indicates the action being inquired about in the question.</div><div><strong>?</strong> This is the standard question mark used to end a question in Spanish, indicating the end of the interrogative sentence.</div>','<h2>Sí, tengo un niño y una niña.</h2><div><strong>Sí</strong> This is the Spanish word for "yes". It confirms a statement or responds affirmatively to a question.</div><div><strong>tengo</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "tener", which means "to have". It indicates the possession or existence of something in relation to the subject, which is "I" in English.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is a Spanish indefinite article in the masculine singular form, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English. It denotes the existence of one unspecified item from a group or category.</div><div><strong>niño</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "boy". The article "un" that comes before it indicates that the speaker has one boy.</div><div><strong>y</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction used to connect words, phrases, or clauses.</div><div><strong>una</strong> This is the Spanish indefinite article in the feminine singular form, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English. Similar to "un", "una" denotes the existence of one unspecified item from a group or category, but it is used before feminine nouns.</div><div><strong>niña</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "girl". Like with "niño", the article "una" indicates that the speaker has one girl.</div>','<h2>¡Oh! ¿Y a dónde quiere ir?</h2><div><strong>¡Oh!</strong> This is an interjection in Spanish used to express surprise or excitement, similar to "Oh!" in English.</div><div><strong>¿Y</strong> This is the Spanish word for "and". It’s often used in questions as a connector to add additional information or a follow-up question.</div><div><strong>a dónde</strong> This is a phrase in Spanish meaning "to where". It’s used in questions to inquire about the destination or direction.</div><div><strong>quiere</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "querer", which means "to want". In the context of a question, it’s asking what someone wants to do.</div><div><strong>ir?</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "ir" in Spanish, which means "to go". When paired with "quiere", it asks where someone wishes or intends to go.</div>','<h2>Sí, tengo un perro y una serpiente.</h2><div><strong>Sí</strong> This is the Spanish word for "yes". It indicates affirmation or agreement to a statement.</div><div><strong>tengo</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "tener", which means "to have" in English. It indicates possession or ownership of something by the speaker.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the Spanish masculine indefinite article, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English, and it’s used here before a masculine noun.</div><div><strong>perro</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "dog". It’s a common pet and here it’s presented as one of the animals the speaker has.</div><div><strong>y</strong> This is the Spanish word for "and". It’s a conjunction used to connect words or phrases of the same part of speech, such as nouns or adjectives.</div><div><strong>una</strong> This is the Spanish feminine indefinite article, matching "a" or "an" in English, and it’s used here before a feminine noun.</div><div><strong>serpiente</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "snake". It’s another type of pet that the speaker is indicating possession of.</div>','<h2>Me encanta escuchar y tocar música.</h2><div><strong>Me</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun in Spanish, often used here as part of a pronominal verb structure that indicates an action that pleases the speaker. It’s equivalent to "to me" or "for me" in this context.</div><div><strong>encanta</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "encantar", which means "to enchant" or "to love" in the context of liking something very much. In this structure, it’s similar to "I love" in English.</div><div><strong>escuchar</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb that means "to listen" or "to hear" in English. In this sentence, it’s used to indicate an activity that the speaker loves to do.</div><div><strong>y</strong> This is the Spanish word for "and". It’s a conjunction used to connect phrases or clauses.</div><div><strong>tocar</strong> This is another Spanish infinitive verb meaning "to play" in the context of playing an instrument or "to touch". Here, it’s used to indicate another activity that the speaker loves to do.</div><div><strong>música</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "music". It’s the object of the activities "escuchar" and "tocar", the things the speaker loves to listen to and play.</div>','<h2>¿Y usted? ¿Qué hace en la vida?</h2><div><strong>¿Y</strong> This is the Spanish word for "and". It’s a conjunction that is often used to connect words or phrases.</div><div><strong>usted?</strong> This is the formal second-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s used in formal contexts or when addressing someone respectfully.</div><div><strong>¿Qué</strong> This is the Spanish interrogative pronoun meaning "what". It is often used to ask questions about objects, actions, or descriptions.</div><div><strong>hace</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "hacer", which means "to do" or "to make". In this question, it refers to the action the person being addressed performs.</div><div><strong>en</strong> This preposition in Spanish translates to "in" in English. It can indicate location, time, or manner.</div><div><strong>la vida?</strong> This Spanish phrase means "in life". "La" is the feminine singular article "the", and "vida" is a feminine noun meaning "life". Together, they ask about what someone does in their life, implying their occupation or main activity.</div>','<h2>Él entró y me dio una caja grande.</h2><div><strong>Él</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>entró</strong> This is the third-person singular past tense form of the verb "entrar", which means "to enter". Here, it indicates that the action of entering happened in the past.</div><div><strong>y</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction used to connect words, phrases, or clauses.</div><div><strong>me</strong> This is a first-person singular indirect object pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "me" in English. It indicates to whom the action of the verb is done.</div><div><strong>dio</strong> This is the third-person singular past tense form of the verb "dar", which means "to give". It indicates that the action of giving happened in the past.</div><div><strong>una</strong> This is an indefinite article in Spanish, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English. It is feminine singular to agree with the noun it modifies.</div><div><strong>caja</strong> This is a feminine singular noun in Spanish that means "box". It is the direct object receiving the action of giving.</div><div><strong>grande</strong> This is an adjective in Spanish meaning "big" or "large". It matches the feminine singular form of the noun it modifies.</div>','<h2>Me gusta verte sonreír y divertirte.</h2><div><strong>Me</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "me" in English. It’s used here as an indirect object of the verb "gustar", which means "to like".</div><div><strong>gusta</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "gustar", which means "to like". It’s used with an indirect object to indicate what pleases someone, in this case, the speaker.</div><div><strong>verte</strong> This is a combination of "ver" (to see) and "te" (you, as a direct object). "Verte" means "to see you", functioning as an infinitive phrase that is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>sonreír</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "sonreír", which means "to smile". It works here as part of the subject of the sentence together with "verte".</div><div><strong>y</strong> This is the Spanish word for "and". It’s a conjunction used to connect words, phrases, or clauses.</div><div><strong>divertirte</strong> This is another combination of a verb and a pronoun: "divertir" (to have fun or to entertain) combined with "te" (you). "Divertirte" means "to have fun" or "to enjoy yourself", and it’s another infinitive phrase that forms the latter part of the compound subject.</div>','<h2>Tomé mi ducha y luego me vestí.</h2><div><strong>Tomé</strong> This is the first-person singular preterite tense form of the verb "tomar", which means "to take" in English. In this context, it means "I took" as in taking an action, referring to the action of showering.</div><div><strong>mi</strong> This is a possessive adjective in Spanish meaning "my". It modifies "ducha", showing possession of the shower.</div><div><strong>ducha</strong> This word refers to "shower" in English. In this sentence, it is the object being taken, indicating that the speaker has taken a shower.</div><div><strong>y</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction used to connect phrases or clauses.</div><div><strong>luego</strong> This is an adverb meaning "then" or "afterwards" in English. It indicates the sequence of events, with taking a shower coming first, followed by the subsequent action.</div><div><strong>me</strong> Before a reflexive verb, "me" is a reflexive pronoun in Spanish that means "myself", indicating that the action is performed by the speaker on themselves.</div><div><strong>vestí</strong> This is the first-person singular preterite tense form of the reflexive verb "vestirse", which means "to dress (oneself)" in English. It indicates that the speaker has completed the action of dressing themselves.</div>','<h2>Muy bien. ¿Desea un café o un té?</h2><div><strong>Muy</strong> In Spanish, "muy" means "very". It is an adverb used to modify adjectives or other adverbs, often intensifying the meaning.</div><div><strong>bien</strong> This is a Spanish adverb meaning "well". It is often used to express that something is in good condition or correct. Here, it could be a response indicating that everything is going well or fine.</div><div><strong>¿Desea</strong> This is the third-person singular present form of the verb "desear", which means "to wish" or "to want". In the context of a question, it’s used to politely inquire about someone’s desire or preference, similar to "Would you like" in English.</div><div><strong>un café</strong> This translates to "a coffee" in English. "Un" is the masculine singular indefinite article equivalent to "a" in English, and "café" is the Spanish word for "coffee".</div><div><strong>o</strong> This is the Spanish conjunction "or", used to present alternatives or choices between two or more items.</div><div><strong>un té</strong> Similarly, this translates to "a tea" in English. Again, "un" is the indefinite article, and "té" means "tea" in Spanish.</div>','<h2>¿Tú prefieres ir a la derecha o a la izquierda?</h2><div><strong>¿</strong> This symbol is the inverted question mark used in Spanish to indicate the beginning of a question.</div><div><strong>Tú</strong> This is the second-person singular subject pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "you" in English.</div><div><strong>prefieres</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "preferir", which means "to prefer".</div><div><strong>ir</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to go" in Spanish.</div><div><strong>a</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish which can be translated as "to" in this context.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the feminine singular definite article in Spanish, meaning "the".</div><div><strong>derecha</strong> This is a noun meaning "right" as in the direction.</div><div><strong>o</strong> This word is a conjunction meaning "or" in English.</div><div><strong>a</strong> This is repeated and it remains a preposition in Spanish translated as "to" in English.</div><div><strong>la</strong> Again, this is the feminine singular definite article in Spanish, meaning "the".</div><div><strong>izquierda</strong> This is a noun meaning "left" as in the direction. </div><div><strong>?</strong> This symbol is the standard question mark used in Spanish to indicate the end of a question.</div>','<h2>¿Recuerda si el ladrón era un hombre o una mujer?</h2><div><strong>¿</strong> This inverted question mark begins a question in Spanish. It is unique to the Spanish language and indicates that what follows is a question.</div><div><strong>Recuerda</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "recordar", which means "to remember". In this context, it is the formal second-person singular, equivalent to "Do you remember" in English.</div><div><strong>si</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish that means "if" or "whether". In this sentence, it introduces a conditional clause.</div><div><strong>el ladrón</strong> This phrase means "the thief" in English. "El" is a masculine singular definite article, and "ladrón" is a masculine noun meaning "thief".</div><div><strong>era</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "ser", which means "to be". It describes a state of being in the past, and in this sentence can be translated to "was" in English.</div><div><strong>un hombre</strong> This phrase translates to "a man" in English. "Un" is the masculine singular indefinite article in Spanish, and "hombre" is the Spanish word for "man".</div><div><strong>o</strong> This is the conjunction "or" in English. It is used to offer an alternative between two choices.</div><div><strong>una mujer</strong> This phrase translates to "a woman" in English. "Una" is the feminine singular indefinite article in Spanish, and "mujer" is the Spanish word for "woman".</div><div><strong>?</strong> This question mark signifies the end of the question in Spanish.</div>','<h2>¿Su orden es para aquí, o para llevar?</h2><div><strong>¿</strong> This symbol indicates that the sentence is a question in Spanish. It is an inverted question mark and is placed at the beginning of any question.</div><div><strong>Su</strong> This is a possessive adjective in Spanish, equivalent to "your" in English. It is used to indicate that something belongs to the person being spoken to in a formal manner (usted).</div><div><strong>orden</strong> This is a noun meaning "order" in English. In the context of the sentence, it refers to what the person has requested or will request, typically in a restaurant or food service establishment.</div><div><strong>es</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "ser", which means "to be". It is used here to link the subject (su orden) to the rest of the sentence, indicating a state of being.</div><div><strong>para</strong> A preposition that can be translated to "for" in English. It is used here twice with different meanings: first to indicate the intended location of consumption ("here"), and second for the purpose of indicating the order will be taken "to go".</div><div><strong>aquí</strong> This is an adverb meaning "here" in English. It indicates the location where the order is intended to be consumed.</div><div><strong>,</strong> This punctuation mark is a comma, which in Spanish, just like in English, indicates a small pause or separates clauses in a list.</div><div><strong>o</strong> This word is a conjunction meaning "or" in English. It is used to offer a choice or alternative between two options.</div><div><strong>para llevar</strong> The phrase "para llevar" can be translated as "to go" or "to take away" in English. It indicates that the order is intended to be taken out of the premises for consumption elsewhere.</div><div><strong>?</strong> This symbol is the standard question mark, indicating the end of a question in Spanish. It places emphasis on the interrogative nature of the sentence.</div>','<h2>¿Es esa tu opinión o un hecho?</h2><div><strong>¿Es</strong> This word is the third-person singular of the verb "ser" in Spanish, which means "to be". It is used here to form a question about the existence or validity of something.</div><div><strong>esa</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective in Spanish referring to "that" in English. It is feminine singular and agrees with the noun it modifies, which is implied in the sentence (opinión or hecho).</div><div><strong>tu</strong> This is the second-person singular possessive adjective in Spanish, equivalent to "your" in English. It indicates that the following noun (opinion) belongs to the person being addressed.</div><div><strong>opinión</strong> This is a noun in Spanish meaning "opinion". It is the idea or belief that the person holds.</div><div><strong>o</strong> This is the Spanish word for "or". It is a conjunction used to connect alternatives.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is an indefinite article in Spanish, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English, used here with the masculine noun "hecho".</div><div><strong>hecho?</strong> This is a noun in Spanish that means "fact". It’s used to reference something that is known to be true or has actually occurred. In the context of the question, it contrasts with "opinión".</div>','<h2>¿Lo quiere para ahora o para más tarde?</h2><div><strong>¿</strong> This is the opening question mark in Spanish, indicating that what follows is a question. In Spanish, questions both open and close with a question mark.</div><div><strong>Lo</strong> This is a direct object pronoun in Spanish, meaning "it" or "him" in English. In this sentence, it refers to something previously mentioned or understood from the context.</div><div><strong>quiere</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "querer", which means "to want" or "to love". In this context, it’s being used to ask if someone wants something.</div><div><strong>para</strong> This is a preposition that translates to "for" in English. It’s used here twice, with different meanings: first, it’s used to indicate a time frame for immediate use, then it indicates a time frame for later use.</div><div><strong>ahora</strong> This is an adverb meaning "now" in English. It’s indicating the option of immediate presence, in this context.</div><div><strong>o</strong> This is the conjunction "or" in English. It’s used to present an alternative or choice between two options.</div><div><strong>para</strong> Again, "para" as a preposition, but here indicating a preference for a later time.</div><div><strong>más tarde?</strong> This phrase means "later" in English. "Más" means "more", and "tarde" translates to "late" or "afternoon". Together, they mean "later", indicating a preference for something to happen at a later time.</div>','<h2>¿Es un carro nuevo o usado?</h2><div><strong>¿Es</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "ser", which means "to be". It indicates either a permanent state or essential qualities.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the indefinite article in Spanish, masculine singular form, equivalent to "a" in English.</div><div><strong>carro</strong> This is the Spanish noun for "car". It can be used synonymously with "coche" in some Spanish-speaking countries. It is masculine and singular to match the article "un".</div><div><strong>nuevo</strong> This is an adjective meaning "new" in Spanish. It agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which in this case is "carro".</div><div><strong>o</strong> This is the Spanish conjunction for "or", used to offer an alternative or choice.</div><div><strong>usado</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "usar", used here as an adjective meaning "used" or "second-hand". Like "nuevo", it agrees in gender and number with the noun "carro".</div>','<h2>Sí, por supuesto. ¿En el bar, o en el comedor?</h2><div><strong>Sí</strong> This is the Spanish word for "yes". It confirms a statement or responds affirmatively to a question.</div><div><strong>por supuesto</strong> This Spanish phrase means "of course". It’s used to express agreement or confirmation without hesitation.</div><div><strong>¿En</strong> This is the Spanish preposition for "in" or "on". It indicates location or position within a place.</div><div><strong>el</strong> This is the Spanish definite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It specifies a particular noun that is previously known to the listener or reader.</div><div><strong>bar</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "bar". It designates a specific place for drinking, often serving snacks or light meals.</div><div><strong>o</strong> This word translates to "or" in English. It’s a conjunction used to link alternatives.</div><div><strong>en</strong> Repeated from earlier, this is the Spanish preposition for "in" or "on". It again indicates location.</div><div><strong>el</strong> Repeated from earlier, this is the Spanish definite article for masculine singular nouns.</div><div><strong>comedor</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "dining room". It refers to a room used for consuming meals.</div>','<h2>¿Desea enviarla por correo regular o expreso?</h2><div><strong>¿</strong> This symbol is the opening question mark used in Spanish to indicate the beginning of a question. Unlike English, Spanish uses both an opening and a closing question mark.</div><div><strong>Desea</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "desear", which means "to wish" or "to want". In this sentence, it can be translated as "Do you want" addressing the second-person formal "you" (usted).</div><div><strong>enviarla</strong> This is a combination of the verb "enviar", which means "to send", and the pronoun "la", which is a direct object pronoun meaning "it" or "her", referring to something feminine that’s already been mentioned. The "la" is attached to the end of the verb in imperative and infinitive forms in Spanish.</div><div><strong>por</strong> This preposition means "by" or "through" in English and is used to indicate the method or means of sending.</div><div><strong>correo</strong> This is a noun meaning "mail" or "post". It refers to the postal service or the system by which letters and packages are delivered.</div><div><strong>regular</strong> This is an adjective meaning "regular" in English, used here to describe the standard or normal type of mail service.</div><div><strong>o</strong> This is the Spanish word for "or". It’s a conjunction used to offer an alternative or choice.</div><div><strong>expreso</strong> This adjective means "express" in English, indicating a faster than usual mail service, typically at an additional cost.</div><div><strong>?</strong> This symbol is the closing question mark used in Spanish to indicate the end of a question.</div>','<h2>También construyó este baño pequeño.</h2><div><strong>También</strong> This is the Spanish adverb for "also" or "as well". It indicates that this action is in addition to others mentioned or implied.</div><div><strong>construyó</strong> This is the third-person singular preterite (simple past) tense form of the verb "construir", which means "to build" or "to construct". So, it refers to a completed action in the past.</div><div><strong>este</strong> This is the Spanish demonstrative adjective for "this". It is used to refer to a noun that is close to the speaker in time or space.</div><div><strong>baño</strong> This is the Spanish noun meaning "bathroom" or "bath". It’s a common noun referring to a place to bathe or use the toilet.</div><div><strong>pequeño</strong> This is a Spanish adjective meaning "small" or "little". In Spanish, adjectives agree with the noun they modify in terms of gender and number; "pequeño" is in its masculine singular form to match "baño".</div>','<h2>También tiene que evitar el alcohol.</h2><div><strong>También</strong> This is a Spanish adverb meaning "also" or "too". It’s used to add information that is similar to what has been mentioned before.</div><div><strong>tiene</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "tener", which means "to have". In this context, it’s used to indicate obligation, similar to "must" or "has to" in English.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This word translates to "that" in English. However, in this context, it’s part of the phrase "tiene que" which means "has to" indicating necessity or obligation.</div><div><strong>evitar</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb meaning "to avoid". It is the action that is necessary or obligated to be taken.</div><div><strong>el</strong> This is the Spanish definite article for masculine singular noun, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>alcohol</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "alcohol". It is the object that needs to be avoided, as stated in the sentence.</div>','<h2>Sí. También quisiera tener una televisión.</h2><div><strong>Sí</strong> This is the Spanish word for "yes". It affirms or agrees with a statement or responds affirmatively to a question.</div><div><strong>También</strong> This is a Spanish adverb meaning "also" or "as well". It is used to add information to the statement that is similar to what has been previously mentioned.</div><div><strong>quisiera</strong> This is the first-person singular conditional tense of the verb "querer", which means "to want". It implies a polite or less direct request or desire, equivalent to "would like" in English.</div><div><strong>tener</strong> This is the infinitive form of the Spanish verb meaning "to have". In this context, it is used after "quisiera" to indicate the action or thing desired.</div><div><strong>una</strong> This is a Spanish indefinite article meaning "a" or "an" in English, and it is feminine and singular to agree with the noun it modifies.</div><div><strong>televisión</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "television". It is the object of the sentence, indicating what the speaker would like to have.</div>','<h2>También le voy a ordenar un examen de sangre.</h2><div><strong>También</strong> This is the Spanish adverb meaning "also" or "too". It is used to add something to what was previously said.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is an indirect object pronoun in Spanish. It often translates to "to him", "to her", or "to you" (formal) in English. In this sentence, it indicates that the action of the verb is being done for or to another person (the one receiving the blood test).</div><div><strong>voy</strong> This comes from the verb "ir", which means "to go". "Voy" is the first-person singular present tense form, meaning "I go" or "I am going". In this context, it’s part of the phrase "voy a" which is used to indicate a future action, similar to the English "going to".</div><div><strong>a</strong> This Spanish preposition usually means "to". When coupled with an infinitive verb, it signifies an immediate future tense, as in "going to [do something]".</div><div><strong>ordenar</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb meaning "to order" or "to prescribe". In this sentence, it’s used with "voy a" to indicate the speaker’s future action of ordering a blood test.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the masculine singular indefinite article in Spanish, meaning "a" or "an".</div><div><strong>examen</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in Spanish that means "exam" or "test".</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a Spanish preposition typically meaning "of" or "from". In this context, it connects "examen" to "sangre" to specify what kind of test it is.</div><div><strong>sangre</strong> This is a feminine singular noun in Spanish that means "blood".</div>','<h2>Una solamente, pero tengo también un equipaje de mano.</h2><div><strong>Una</strong> This is the feminine singular form of the number one in Spanish, it often translates to "one" in English. Here it is implied that it modifies a feminine singular noun which is omitted in the conversation, such as "maleta" or "bolsa".</div><div><strong>solamente</strong> This is an adverb in Spanish that means "only" or "just". It is being used to limit the amount described by "una".</div><div><strong>pero</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish that translates to "but" in English. It’s used to introduce a contrasting phrase that follows it.</div><div><strong>tengo</strong> This comes from the Spanish verb "tener", which means "to have". "Tengo" is the first-person singular present form, so it means "I have".</div><div><strong>también</strong> This is an adverb that means "also" or "as well" in English, indicating an addition.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the masculine singular indefinite article in Spanish, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>equipaje</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in Spanish that translates to "luggage" in English.</div><div><strong>de mano</strong> This is a Spanish phrase that means "of hand", which, when combined with "equipaje", forms the phrase "hand luggage" or "carry-on" in English.</div>','<h2>Sí, también estudio mis clases de español con Ouino.</h2><div><strong>Sí</strong> This is the Spanish word for "yes". It affirms a statement or responds positively to a question.</div><div><strong>también</strong> This Spanish adverb means "also" or "as well". It’s used to add information that is similar to what has been said before.</div><div><strong>estudio</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "estudiar", which means "to study". Here, it indicates the action that the speaker is currently doing.</div><div><strong>mis</strong> This is an possessive adjective in Spanish that means "my". It is plural here because it modifies the plural noun "clases".</div><div><strong>clases</strong> This is the plural form of the Spanish noun "clase", which means "class" or "lesson". It refers to the sessions or courses that the speaker is taking.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish which is often translated as "of" or "from". It is used here to show possession, meaning "of" as in "my classes of Spanish".</div><div><strong>español</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "Spanish". It refers to the subject matter of the classes mentioned in the sentence.</div><div><strong>con</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that means "with". It indicates the company or the tool used in conjunction with the action mentioned.</div><div><strong>Ouino</strong> This is a proper noun, which is the name of a language-learning software. In this context, it indicates that the speaker uses Ouino to study Spanish.</div>','<h2>¡Gracias! Tú también. ¡Hasta pronto Tomás!</h2><div><strong>¡Gracias!</strong> This is a common Spanish phrase that means "Thank you!" It is an expression of gratitude.</div><div><strong>Tú</strong> This is the second-person singular subject pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s used to address someone directly in an informal way.</div><div><strong>también</strong> This word translates to "also" or "too" in English. It’s an adverb indicating that the speaker wishes to return the sentiment expressed. In this context, it would mean "You too".</div><div><strong>¡Hasta</strong> This word means "until" in English. It is often used in farewells to mean "until we see each other again".</div><div><strong>pronto</strong> This Spanish adverb means "soon". When used with "hasta", it conveys the meaning of "see you soon" or "until soon".</div><div><strong>Tomás!</strong> This is a proper noun, a person’s name. In this context, it indicates that the speaker is addressing someone named Tomás and saying goodbye to them. The exclamation marks emphasize the interjection, indicating enthusiasm, warmth, or emphasis in the farewell.</div>','<h2>¿También la tarjeta postal?</h2><div><strong>¿</strong> This symbol is the Spanish opening question mark. It’s used at the beginning of a question and informs the reader that a question is being introduced.</div><div><strong>También</strong> This word translates to "also" or "too" in English. It’s an adverb that indicates the addition of something else to what has already been mentioned.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the definite article in Spanish, which translates to "the" in English. It’s feminine and singular to agree with the noun it modifies, "tarjeta postal".</div><div><strong>tarjeta</strong> This noun means "card" in English. In this context, it refers to a type of card that is typically sent in the mail.</div><div><strong>postal</strong> This word functions as an adjective modifying "tarjeta". It translates to "postal" in English, which refers to the sending or receiving of mail. Here, it specifies the type of card being referred to as a "postcard".</div>'];definitions = definitions.concat(defBonus);